module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.one_refusal_two_destinations

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data one_refusal_two_destinations: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "one_refusal_two_destinations" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**one refusal, two destinations, remedy misdirected** (a single refusal at a single source column emits two rows naming DIFFERENT expected types, because two producers resolve the same declared spelling in different environments. ",

    "The reader repairs from the row that names the parameter -- and that is the row the checker did NOT refuse on. ",

    "**THE SYMPTOM IS THE RECOGNITION RULE, NOT THE CAUSE**: what a debugging author encounters is one column, two rows, two expected types; the instruction that saves them is DO NOT REPAIR FROM THE INHABITANCE ROW -- the compat row is what refused. ",

    "Specimen, measured 2026-09-01 on gunbc c0a378c07be by calm-boar-314 and bold-carp-449 independently, on two binaries and two phases (`gunbc compile` emit-stage refusal and `gunbc run` entry-resolve), unrepaired at authoring. ",

    "A module declaring `type String = FreeMonoid<Char>` and `fn takes_bare(s: String) -> Int`, called from a foreign module that IMPORTS the function, with a deliberately wrong `Int` actual so the diagnostic must name its destination, refuses with: `type mismatch: expected 'Primitive(String)', got 'Primitive(Int)'` AND `value does not inhabit its declared type at the direct call argument for parameter 's': declared 'Node(String<Product(Char)>)', produced 'Primitive(Int)'`. ",

    "THE TWO PRODUCERS, read from source rather than inferred from message shape: `v1.compiler.infer direct_call_arg_mismatch_diags` renders the compat row from the formal as the CALLER environment resolves it, and `v1.compiler.infer declared_type_obligation_diags` renders the inhabitance row from `obligation.declared` through `declared_type_inhabitance`. ",

    "Both reach `type_mismatch_error` and `DeclaredTypeNotInhabited` in `v1.compiler.core`, so the rendering is shared and the DIVERGENCE IS UPSTREAM OF IT -- which is why the two rows look like one mechanism disagreeing with itself. ",

    "**THE TRIGGER IS TWO COMPETING BINDINGS LIVE IN ONE RESOLVING SCOPE, NOT THE IMPORT AS SUCH**, and this row states the narrower claim deliberately: the with/without-import pair is measured, but the two AGREEING cells agree for OPPOSITE reasons -- an un-imported foreign caller agrees at `Primitive(String)` because only the kernel binding is available, and an in-module caller agrees at `Node(String<Product(Char)>)` because only the local declaration is. ",

    "Phrasing the trigger as the import would make a reader conclude the class does not reproduce when some other construct puts a second binding in scope. ",

    "An explicit `import <module> \{ <fn> \}` is the ONLY construct measured to create the competition; whether any other does is UNMEASURED. ",

    "Crossed arm, pre-registered before its log was read: removing the import moves ONLY the inhabitance row, from `Node(String<Product(Char)>)` to `Primitive(String)`, while the compat row is `Primitive(String)` under both -- so one producer follows the import binding and the other does not, rather than the whole reading shifting. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1 (mitigatable) -- the line DOES stop, and stops located, so this is not silent wrongness; what fails is that the refusal MISDIRECTS the remedy, which is DESIGN section 5's requirement that a refusal be typed AND located read one layer deeper: located at the right column while naming the wrong destination. ",

    "CEILING: 3 (structurally guaranteed) -- one resolution of a callee's declared type per call site, consumed by both producers, makes two destinations unconstructible. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the capability rather than an artifact: a single resolved-formal authority at the call site that both the compat check and the inhabitance obligation READ, sufficient that no call-site refusal can render two different expected types for one parameter. ",

    "Distinct from `meaning_fork`, which is one NAME carrying two meanings across scopes -- that is the underlying condition here and is separately rostered; this row is about two PRODUCERS disagreeing inside a single refusal, which is what makes the remedy wrong rather than merely the meaning ambiguous. ",

    "Distinct from `diagnostic_name_mechanism_silent`: there the diagnostic names a mechanism that never ran; here both mechanisms ran and both reported faithfully about different environments. ",

    "WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED, by either measurer: which producer is correct, and why they read the binding differently. ",

    "No arm on either rig touches it, and the source reading above identifies the producers without settling which environment a call-argument formal SHOULD be resolved in.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
